WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.894 --> 00:00:03.310
JEFF DAVIS: Welcome to the
Google Cloud Platform Education

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:04.330
Series.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.790
My name is Jeff Davis,
and in this session,

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:09.760
we'll discuss building high
performance, scalable VM

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.670
environments on
Google Compute Engine.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.380
We'll be doing a moderated
Q&amp;A throughout the session,

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.560
so feel free to submit
your questions with the Ask

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.870
Your Question Here field at
the bottom of your screen.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.150
Let's get started.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:23.920
Here's our agenda.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.920
We're going to begin
with an in-depth demo

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.080
where we build the
necessary infrastructure

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.380
to support a fairly
sophisticated application.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.730
This will illustrate all of
the key features within GCE

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.830
and give you an
idea of how quickly

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.490
and how easily you can build
a robust, scalable high

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:45.240
performing infrastructure.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.850
We'll then go ahead and talk
about the wide range of GCE

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.420
features, giving more
details on the features

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.510
that we demonstrated and talking
about some other capabilities

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.810
that may be useful
in your applications

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:00.060
for your particular use cases.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.220
I'll spend a few
minutes describing

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.246
how best to find information
as you're doing research

00:01:06.246 --> 00:01:07.620
after today's
session, and you're

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.330
exploring GCE on your own.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:16.020
We'll talk about strategies
for determining how well GCE

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.680
applies to particular challenges
or particular computing

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.900
workloads that you're looking
to move into the cloud.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.770
And as I mentioned, we'll
be doing a rolling Q&amp;A,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.300
so feel free to keep
those questions coming.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.960
So let's dive into a demo.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.890
So we're going to start
with an application run

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.990
by a company called
Betterdev.IO.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.510
This is the bdev squared system.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:41.550
It's a learning
management system

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.880
that allows employees
all around the world

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.900
to find useful tidbits
provided by other developers

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.560
and get up to speed on
the newest technologies.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.500
You can see down here that
you can easily take a look

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.090
at existing learning paths.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.130
This information is
pulled from a database

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.850
of plans or playlists of
modules that developers

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.760
would find interesting
on particular topics.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:10.690
You can find individual
modules, so for instance, here's

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.450
a series of modules on how
to teach the new Google Cloud

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.780
Platform class, as well as
something about mantas, which

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.890
we'll look at in just a moment.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.220
You can also look at
your learning activity

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.750
and your accomplishments
and where

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.230
you are in your performance
against various plans.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.610
So if you click through
to one of the modules,

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.690
you can see that it's a
small micro learning nugget.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:38.960
It has some text, and it
has, most importantly,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.490
a video segment.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.700
These modules are created
by Better Dev employees,

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.890
and they're shared with
other Better Dev employees,

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:48.980
and then they're put together
into learning plans or learning

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.550
paths that people can follow.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:57.190
If you want to add a
new module, you simply

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:03.710
enter the title, so I Like
Mantas, and I'm the author,

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:07.730
and Mantas, mantas, mantas.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.320
And then you can choose
to upload a video file.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:18.230
What this will do is actually
upload the file to a back end

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.570
and then create a new module
record in the database.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.070
And this module will then
be available to learners

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.490
throughout Better Dev.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.760
You can see that the new
module has appeared here,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.040
and if you click on
any module again,

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.890
it shows you something
about mantas, for instance.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:40.130
Now, some considerations when
we are running this learning

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.340
management system--
we have Better Dev

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.730
have employees all
around the world,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.060
and so we want them to
have a good experience.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.790
Whether they're coming from Asia
or from Europe or from North

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.440
America, we want the
performance to be good.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.970
Additionally, we want
the video to stream

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.490
well regardless of your network
connection or your device.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.460
So those videos
that we upload, they

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.730
may start as 4K or
1080p videos, but we're

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.870
going to want to transcode
them into various sizes

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:13.290
so that we can do adaptive
streaming on the fly.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.070
So let's take a look at the
architecture behind this,

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:20.399
and then we'll actually show you
how to build it from scratch.

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:22.220
So here's the architecture.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.770
We have users coming
from North America,

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.080
from Europe, and from Asia.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.350
They all hit a
single IP address,

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.370
and this IP address is actually
an intelligent load balancer.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:37.580
It will direct users
to the closest set

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.220
of servers that have capacity.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.050
So you'll notice that we
have servers in US Central,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:47.330
in EMEA West, and in Asia East.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.910
We're running
NGINX servers here,

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:54.560
and these servers deliver
the standard user interface

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.100
to the LMS users.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:02.540
If load goes up, we want this
to actually scale and give us

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.660
multiple servers automatically.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.150
These servers are pulling
all of the configuration

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:11.330
data, the user information, the
information about the learning

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.430
paths, the information
about the learning modules,

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.650
out of a Cloud SQL instance.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:20.640
When you create a new
module, you upload media,

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.330
and that media goes into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.780
And as I said before, we
want to transcode that media,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.960
so we want to actually take
the original video file,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.390
and we want to encode it
at 480p, 720p, and 1080p.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.820
So once you dump it
in Cloud Storage,

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.159
we have transcoding servers.

00:05:40.159 --> 00:05:42.450
These are back end servers,
separate from the front end

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.540
servers running NGINX, and
these transcoding servers

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.860
will open up the submitted
file, transcode it

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.610
into different
versions, and store it

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.660
in a different location.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.620
Let's take a look at how
to build this actually

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.600
in Compute Engine.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.050
So if we switch over to my
list of VMs in Compute Engine,

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:05.760
you can see that I have a
single back end instance that

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:07.140
is currently running.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:10.260
This is the server that
watches for new media files

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.530
to be submitted, automatically
grabs those files,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:17.100
and transcodes them and stores
them in a Google Cloud Storage

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.920
bucket.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:21.740
I also then have
front end instances,

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:24.140
and you can see that I've got
three front end instances.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.030
I have one in each
of the regions.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.060
I have Asia,
Europe, and US West,

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:32.630
so traffic will get directed
to each of these regions

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:35.360
based on where the traffic
originates and whether or not

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.530
the target region has
sufficient capacity to serve

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:42.400
the additional requests.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.070
I have a network load balancer,
so if I go to the Networking

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:48.160
section, you can see that
there is a single load

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.050
balancer that is configured.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.950
This load balancer
provides the IP address

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:58.030
that we're hitting that then
directs users to the closest

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.100
available resource.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.690
We have Google Cloud
Storage set up,

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.930
and you can see that
we have raw media.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.240
These are the files
that are submitted

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.330
by developers who
are creating modules

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.220
to share with their peers.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.680
And then we have
another bucket here,

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.270
and this bucket contains
transcoded video files.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:22.390
These are, for instance,
the for 480p, the 720p,

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.210
and the 1080p versions
of videos that have been

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:28.120
uploaded by module creators.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.730
There's also a
Cloud SQL instance,

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:33.700
and this is the database
that is containing

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.760
all of the user learning
path and learning

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.453
module information.

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:46.180
So let's test before
we take this all apart

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.220
and try to build it
again from scratch.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:51.250
Let's test the
load distribution,

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:53.380
the geographic
routing of traffic,

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:56.230
and let's test the autoscaling.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:58.840
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to bring up three test

00:07:58.840 --> 00:07:59.470
machines.

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:01.410
I have a machine
in Asia-Pacific,

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:04.410
a machine in EMEA, and
a machine in the US.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:12.300
I am going to s-sh into
each one of these in turn,

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.840
and I'm going to do a little
load generation on each one.

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:29.070
Let's see, shrink
this one over here.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:33.804
So you can see that I've
got three s-sh sessions.

00:08:33.804 --> 00:08:36.610
Make this a little bigger so
that everybody can see it.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:44.140
OK.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:46.660
Now, again, the site
that we're going to

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:50.290
is the site represented
by this IP address.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:55.660
So I'm going to grab
that IP address,

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:58.420
and let's do something simple.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.260
Let's use Apache Bench.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:08.390
We're going to do
5,000 requests.

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:11.720
We're going to have one
simultaneous session,

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:15.130
and we're going to just
pull the home page.

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:17.120
And I want to take
that command, and I

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.730
want to run it in three
different locations.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.500
I want to run it
in Asia-Pacific.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.750
I want to run it in the US,
and I want to run it in EMEA.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:32.000
Now, we also want
to see the effect

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:33.980
that this low generation has.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.540
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.070
going to look at our back
end service configuration,

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:43.660
and this will give us a nice
little chart that shows us

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.040
how traffic is getting routed.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:48.670
This shows us the origination
region of the traffic,

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:51.550
and this shows us what
back end the traffic is

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:54.130
getting routed to.

00:09:54.130 --> 00:09:58.600
So with that, what we'll do
is we'll kick off the traffic

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:11.500
sessions on each one of these,
and then we will go back

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:20.870
to back end services.

00:10:23.584 --> 00:10:25.000
And what we should
see here-- now,

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.780
this is a graphical
representation that tells you

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:31.600
about the health
of your back ends

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.570
as well as the traffic that's
being directed to them.

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:37.210
This doesn't really
update in real time,

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:41.050
so I'm going to sort of
force it to refresh here.

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:42.700
But when we're done,
what we should see

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.070
is traffic
originating from three

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:48.040
different geographic
regions, and the traffic

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:51.385
is getting routed to servers
also in those regions.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.260
Voila.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:59.300
So you can see that
we've got requests

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:00.860
coming from Asia going to Asia.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:03.380
We've got requests coming
from North America going

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:04.190
to North America.

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:07.580
And we've got requests coming
from Europe going to Europe.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:10.580
You'll notice that we're
already actually reaching

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:16.370
the maximum utilization that
I've set on these instance

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:20.300
groups, so that really, we
don't want servers handling

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:22.410
more than 50
requests per second,

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:24.410
and this is handling 67.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:26.150
This is handling 55.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:28.040
So what should
also happen here is

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.060
we should see some autoscaling.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:31.640
So the instance
groups will actually

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:34.760
add servers to deal with the
additional capacity requests.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:42.420
So I'm going to refresh
my browser in hopes

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:43.950
that I can actually get--

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:45.630
you can see, for
instance, Asia has now

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:48.660
scaled up to three instances.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:51.930
You can see that North America
and EMEA are scaling up

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:52.711
to four.

00:11:52.711 --> 00:11:54.960
This says that there are
instances that are unhealthy.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:58.440
This is probably just because
they're still spinning up,

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:02.240
and they haven't started
handling requests yet.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.300
If we go back to Compute Engine,
and we go to Instance Groups,

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.640
you can look at the
instance groups,

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:10.700
and you can see that they're
scaling up currently.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:15.590
So for instance,
if I look at Asia,

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:18.950
I can see that I'm
averaging about 20% CPU

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:22.220
utilization across
all of the instances,

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:27.230
and that there are three live
instances responding to load.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:30.350
If I go to EMEA, we
should see that I'm

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.230
now actually up to four
instances handling the load,

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:36.620
and again, this has kept the
CPU utilization, on average,

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:38.150
down to around 20%.

00:12:41.164 --> 00:12:42.080
So that's pretty cool.

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:46.970
What we have is a situation
where increased traffic drives

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:49.700
automatic scaling
of my instances,

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:53.990
and the traffic gets routed
to the nearest instance

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:55.522
group that has capacity.

00:12:55.522 --> 00:12:57.230
You'll notice most of
the stuff from Asia

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.000
goes directly to Asia.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.840
Occasionally, there may be
requests where the Asia cluster

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:05.150
is determined to not
have enough capacity,

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:08.690
so it automatically routes
it to the next lowest cost

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:13.230
network path, which in
this case would be Europe.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.720
So that shows scaling
of the front end.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:20.520
Let's take a quick look at
scaling on the back end.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:24.780
So to check out scaling on the
back end, what we need to do

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:30.160
is we need to create several new
modules and upload new videos.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:33.420
The video transcoding
will eat all of the CPU

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:37.110
for a back end server, so
if we do multiple uploads,

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.610
were going to need
multiple back end

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.050
servers to do the transcoding.

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:42.450
So the instance script that
we want to look at here

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:45.780
is actually the back
end group, and we're

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:49.110
going to look at it
by CPU utilization.

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:52.900
And let's add a couple
of additional modules.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:14:02.510
Here's a demo module, and I'm
going to pick a video file

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:05.580
and upload that.

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:09.920
And I'm going to create another
module, and this will be Demo2.

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:17.910
And we'll pick
another video file.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:23.560
And we can do one more, Demo3.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:35.540
So now, what we've done is
we've uploaded three new video

00:14:35.540 --> 00:14:40.280
files, each of which needs to
be transcoded, and you can see,

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:42.917
here's the first module.

00:14:42.917 --> 00:14:44.500
If I click on this,
this will actually

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:48.460
take me to a learning module
that has an embedded video.

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:51.460
This is the raw 1080p video,
though, On the back end, what

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:55.010
we want to have happen is we
want the transcoding to occur.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:59.380
If we look in the bucket behind
the scenes, what you'll see

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:04.260
is there are new-- there's the
Chapter 1, 2, and 3 videos.

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:10.620
And we want to have
more new transcoded

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:12.070
sessions appear here.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:21.230
So you'll notice that this is
my back end instance group,

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:24.170
and you can see, now, instead
of having one instance,

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:25.610
there are three instances.

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:28.130
And you can see that the
average CPU utilization

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:31.160
is on the order of 80%--

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:32.960
75%, 80%.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:34.670
And so what this has
done is, every time

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:38.460
I upload a new module
that contains a video,

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.410
a message goes to
the back end servers.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.700
The back end servers kick
off the transcoding sessions.

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:46.350
The transcoding sessions
shoot the CPU utilization

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:47.490
through the roof.

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:50.950
Because I've set a limit on
the amount of CPU utilization

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:55.650
I'm comfortable taking on, when
I go above 60% CPU utilization,

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:57.660
it's builds up another
instance, and that then

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:02.260
pulls the next video for
transcoding out of the bucket.

00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:04.740
And so this whole thing
is working for us.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:08.950
Now, it looks great,
you say, Jeff, terrific.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:10.700
How hard is this to build?

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:12.400
Were you up until
4:00 AM this morning

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:13.520
putting this all together?

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:16.720
And the answer is maybe,
but you won't have to be.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:18.460
It's actually quite
straightforward.

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:20.350
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:24.960
going to start with two bases.

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:28.650
What I've got is a back end
base and a front end base,

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:33.000
and these are simply standard
Debian images onto which

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.547
I have installed some software.

00:16:36.547 --> 00:16:38.130
I could do that all
from scratch, now,

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:40.213
but it takes a little bit
of time to set those up.

00:16:40.213 --> 00:16:41.910
What I'm going to
do, though, is I

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:47.280
am going to take each of these
preconfigured virtual machines,

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:50.670
and I am going to create
a base image from them.

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:57.206
So you can see, I don't
want to delete the boot disk

00:16:57.206 --> 00:16:58.330
when I delete the instance.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:00.205
I'm going to check each
of those to make sure

00:17:00.205 --> 00:17:01.175
that that's the case.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:11.270
So what we're going to do
then is these two base images

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:14.869
that are configured to work just
the way I want-- what I'm going

00:17:14.869 --> 00:17:17.310
to do now is I'm
going to delete them,

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:21.089
but I will keep
their boot disks.

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:25.020
You always start this recipe
with a preconfigured machine

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:27.480
that works the way
you want it to.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.780
Then what you can do is
create a boot disk image.

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:36.100
So I'm going to
create a new image,

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:39.430
and I'm going to call
this Demo Front End.

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:41.680
And you pick a source--

00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:43.420
in this case is
an existing disk.

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:47.260
This is a disk that I took
off of a functioning VM.

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:49.840
And I'm going to
pick the FE base,

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:52.134
and I'm going to create
a new disk image.

00:17:52.134 --> 00:17:53.800
Now, I'm doing this
all through the GUI.

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:56.680
You can just as easily do
this from the command line

00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:59.920
or you can script it or you
can write custom management

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.190
tools that will automate this
entire process for you if you

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:04.600
like.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:07.700
So while that's creating
the front end image,

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:11.620
I'm also going to
create a back end image,

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.920
and I'm going to call
this Demo Back End,

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:16.660
and again, it's going
to start with a disk.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:19.990
And I'm going to go to
the LMS back end base,

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:22.060
and I get to create that image.

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:25.720
So when I'm done, I should
have two new boot disk

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:30.100
images, created from the
disks, on two functioning VM

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:30.900
instances.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:37.480
Once we get that
up and running--

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:38.980
let me refresh this.

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:42.180
You can see that my two
images are being created.

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:44.550
Once I have these
images, I can then

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:48.720
create new VM instances
that look just

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:52.820
like the original development
machines that I was working on.

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:54.570
And that's great, but
I don't want to just

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:56.430
build them one at a time.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.580
I actually want to have Google
Compute Engine automatically

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:01.830
create these instances
and be able to scale up

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:04.560
the number of instances or scale
down the number of instances

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:06.580
for me automatically.

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:08.430
So to do that, I
need to create what's

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.580
called an instance template.

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:13.260
So there's my first image done.

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:15.060
My second image is finishing up.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:18.270
So I'm going to go to
Instance Templates,

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:21.120
and I'm going to create
a new instance template,

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:26.250
and we'll call this
Demo Front End Template.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:29.400
And I'm going to pick a standard
machine configuration, so

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:33.520
one virtual CPU and 3.7
gigabytes of memory.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:36.200
I'm not going to do a
clean Debian install.

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:37.950
Instead, I'm going to
pick a custom image,

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:42.380
and this is going to be
my demo front end image.

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.180
And I'm going to designate
the scopes available.

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:47.750
So when you boot
up a VM, you can

00:19:47.750 --> 00:19:51.380
designate whether it has
access to a certain APIs.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:53.270
We're going to go ahead
and set that and say,

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:56.660
you know what, I need
access to Pub/Sub.

00:19:56.660 --> 00:20:00.140
I need access to Cloud SQL.

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:09.667
I need access to Compute Engine,
and I need access to storage.

00:20:09.667 --> 00:20:10.750
So where's Compute Engine?

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:11.960
There we go.

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:15.410
So I set the scopes, and
what this is basically saying

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:20.300
is any time I boot
up an instance based

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.550
on this template,
I want to make sure

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:24.890
that it's using
this boot image, I

00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:26.690
want to make sure it's
this machine type,

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:30.050
and I want to make sure that
it has access to these APIs.

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:34.860
So I'm going to go ahead
and create the template,

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:40.062
and then I need to similarly
create a back end template.

00:20:40.062 --> 00:20:42.270
Now, the back end template
requires a little bit more

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:44.100
configuration.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:46.460
I need to actually
grab a startup script,

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:50.830
so I'm going to look at one
of the existing instances,

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:54.280
and I am going to go
find the startup script.

00:20:54.280 --> 00:20:56.290
So you can see here,
this is the script

00:20:56.290 --> 00:21:00.100
that runs when the back end
instance server boots up.

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:03.555
So I'm going to go ahead and
create an instance template,

00:21:03.555 --> 00:21:05.430
and this one is going
to be for the back end,

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:10.110
so we'll call it Demo
Back End Template.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:11.980
Same machine configuration.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:15.180
Again, I'm not going to
use a standard image.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:17.340
I'm going to use a custom image.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:20.400
It's the demo back end.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:24.380
And again, I need to specify
what scopes it's using,

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:27.770
and it's going to be
using cloud Pub/Sub,

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:31.880
and it's going to be using
the Compute Engine API,

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:36.190
and it's going to be using
the Cloud Storage API.

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:39.430
And then I'm going to
specify which startup

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:43.180
script it runs when it boots.

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:44.620
And we'll create that.

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:47.880
So what we've done
is we took stock VMs,

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:49.950
and we added our applications.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:52.590
And that created
newly configured VMs.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:54.180
We took those VMs.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:56.250
We copied their disks
and made images.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:59.460
We took those images, and
now, we've made templates.

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:04.050
From the templates, we're
going to make instance groups.

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:06.030
So I'm going to create
a new instance group,

00:22:06.030 --> 00:22:08.550
and an instance group
is a collection of VMs

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:10.542
that you can manage together.

00:22:10.542 --> 00:22:12.000
And if they're
based on a template,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:15.730
you can set up autoscaling and
load balancing quite easily.

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:20.020
So we're going to do Demo
Front End North America,

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:23.290
and I'm going to put these
all in the West zone,

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:25.150
and I'm going to use
an instance template.

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:27.640
This is going to be
the front end template.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:30.220
And I'm going to
turn autoscaling on,

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:33.760
and I want autoscaling to be
based on HTTP load balancing

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.140
usage.

00:22:35.140 --> 00:22:39.730
And I want to set a minimum
of one instance running and up

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:42.890
to five instances running.

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:44.030
So that is my--

00:22:47.202 --> 00:22:49.410
actually, we'll come back
we'll do the load balancing

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:50.118
in just a second.

00:22:50.118 --> 00:22:52.050
We'll start with CPU usage.

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:55.980
We'll create this
instance group.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.450
Now, for the front
ends, remember

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:59.100
we had them globally
distributed,

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:01.230
so I'm going to create--

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:02.880
I'll just create one more.

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:06.150
And this will be
Demo Front End EMEA,

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:09.180
and I'm going to set
this to run in Europe.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:13.960
There we go.

00:23:13.960 --> 00:23:17.710
And again, I'm going to
use the same template,

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:20.140
and I'm going to
set autoscaling on.

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:25.380
And this is going to go from one
to five, and we'll create that.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:28.730
So now I've created
two instance groups--

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:31.310
one for the front end
in North America, one

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:32.870
for the front end
in EMEA, and I need

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:36.020
to create one more, which
is for the back end.

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:37.820
So this will be Demo Back End--

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:42.770
we'll just call that
Demo Back End Group,

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:48.710
and we're going to
put that in US West.

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:52.520
And we're going to use the
Demo Back End Template.

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:56.660
We're going to set autoscaling
on based on CPU utilization,

00:23:56.660 --> 00:23:59.390
from one to five, and
we're going to create that.

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:08.340
So now, what we have is
we have an instance group

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:12.070
for the back end, and that
runs in North America.

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.580
And we have two instance
groups for the front ends-- one

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:16.500
in North America
and one in EMEA.

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:22.010
If you look at the
VMs, what you'll see

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:26.000
is we have one instance
for the front end in EMEA,

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:28.950
one instance for the front
end in North America,

00:24:28.950 --> 00:24:33.050
and one instance spinning
up for the back end that's

00:24:33.050 --> 00:24:36.482
also running in North America.

00:24:36.482 --> 00:24:37.940
So you can filter
here, and you can

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:39.920
see these are the three
instances that are currently

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:40.510
running.

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:43.100
Again, these are
autoscaling instance groups,

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:45.350
so they can go up to
five instances per,

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:48.950
but because there's no
load on them currently,

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:51.147
there's only one
running in each group.

00:24:51.147 --> 00:24:52.730
Now, the next thing
that we need to do

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:54.080
is set up the load balancer.

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:55.880
This is the single
IP address that's

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:58.250
going to send
traffic intelligently

00:24:58.250 --> 00:25:00.620
to the right back end,
depending on where

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:02.670
the request originates.

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:06.140
So in order to do that,
what we're going to do

00:25:06.140 --> 00:25:10.520
is switch over to Networking,
and in Networking I

00:25:10.520 --> 00:25:14.020
am going to click
Load Balancing,

00:25:14.020 --> 00:25:16.502
and I'm going to create
a new load balancer.

00:25:16.502 --> 00:25:18.460
Notice there are three
types of load balancers.

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:21.010
I'm going to pick an
HTTP load balancer.

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:23.800
You start by giving
it a name, so this is

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:26.740
going to be Demo Load Balancer.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:29.780
You configure a back end
service, and to be clear,

00:25:29.780 --> 00:25:32.540
this is a back end
load balancing service.

00:25:32.540 --> 00:25:35.470
This is not the back end
service for my application.

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:37.840
So I'm going to create
the back end service,

00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:44.820
and I'm going to call this
Demo Front End Load Balancing,

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:50.870
and I'm going to pick the North
American front end instance

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:51.710
group.

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:55.970
I'm going to say
if I'm at 30% CPU--

00:25:55.970 --> 00:25:59.550
or if I'm at 80% CPU utilization
or 30 requests per second,

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:04.430
then consider this group of
servers to be overcapacity,

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:07.160
and then I'm going to do
the same thing for Europe.

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:15.030
And let's see-- don't need that.

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:16.710
And then I need to
set a health check,

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:20.010
and the health check is going to
be the Front End Health Check.

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:23.660
So now, my back end service for
the load balancer is set up.

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:25.650
My host path and rules,
I don't need to set.

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:27.150
My front end
configuration-- this

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:29.790
is going to be an
HTTP load balancer,

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:32.070
and it's going to
use ephemeral ports,

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:35.940
and I can go ahead then and
create the load balancer.

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:38.130
So while this spins
up in the background,

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:41.700
we're going to cross our fingers
and hope that, when we're done,

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:44.880
we click on the IP address
for the load balancer.

00:26:44.880 --> 00:26:46.920
It's going to
automatically determine

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:49.764
which back end group of
servers to send us to.

00:26:49.764 --> 00:26:51.930
We're going to go to that
back end group of servers,

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:53.790
and one of those
servers will respond.

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:57.750
And if we generate load,
the server instance groups

00:26:57.750 --> 00:26:59.980
should automatically
scale for us.

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:01.620
So let's take a look.

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:04.740
So we cross our fingers
and hope that this actually

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:06.190
is going to work.

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:11.970
Here's our global load balancing
pointing at our back end

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:13.800
instance groups for the demo.

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:15.300
That's what we just created.

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:18.419
We click on this,
and I'll be darned.

00:27:18.419 --> 00:27:18.960
Look at that.

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:21.060
We can see that this is
actually being served

00:27:21.060 --> 00:27:25.010
by a demo front end
server, and it's

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:29.450
being served out of
the US West 1A zone,

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:34.370
and it has access to all
of the same information

00:27:34.370 --> 00:27:37.580
that the previous incarnation
of the application

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:39.530
was using because it's
talking to the same SQL

00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:41.580
instance in the background.

00:27:41.580 --> 00:27:44.560
If I want to
generate some load--

00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:50.800
let's say I want to generate
5,000 sessions with 50

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:52.610
simultaneous sessions.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:58.090
I'm going to do that, and I
can do the same thing in EMEA.

00:27:58.090 --> 00:28:04.630
And hopefully, what we'll see
is that this generates traffic

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:08.210
across our back end servers.

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:10.870
So let's go back end services.

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:14.860
We can see that this is
the previous back end

00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:18.380
service that we had configured
for the original application.

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.300
This is our demo application.

00:28:20.300 --> 00:28:22.930
If we click on this,
what we should see

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:26.990
is traffic going to--

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:32.340
again, we need to
wait for it to update,

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:38.560
but we do see traffic
going from being originated

00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:41.290
in North America and in Europe.

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:45.160
And over time, the visualization
should get cleaned up,

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:49.450
and you should actually see
some of this going to Europe.

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:54.060
Let's take a look at the
instance groups real quick

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:56.410
and see how they're doing.

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:59.730
So we have the demo
instance groups.

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.550
They are all at one, currently.

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:04.140
They haven't needed
to autoscale yet,

00:29:04.140 --> 00:29:06.420
but they will
autoscale as necessary.

00:29:06.420 --> 00:29:09.300
So in the course of about
five to seven minutes,

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:12.330
we were able to
essentially replicate

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:14.190
the same sort of
configuration that we

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:19.470
started with by creating images
from good virtual machines,

00:29:19.470 --> 00:29:22.260
taking the images and
creating instance templates,

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:24.750
taking the instance templates,
creating instance groups,

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:29.490
and then front ending that
with a network load balancer.

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:32.040
And that gives us
the configuration

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:36.700
that we talked about in
the architecture slide.

00:29:36.700 --> 00:29:40.420
So a lot of details coming at
you real fast, but hopefully,

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:44.830
that gives you an idea of
the range of capabilities

00:29:44.830 --> 00:29:50.140
and just how easy it is to
build high performing, scalable,

00:29:50.140 --> 00:29:52.150
globally distributed
applications

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:54.340
on top of Compute Engine.

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:56.170
Let's take a step
back and make sure

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:59.290
that we can put all of those
individual examples in context

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:03.330
and look at Compute Engine's
overall functionality.

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:06.710
So by now, it should be
clear that Compute Engine is

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:10.490
a product that allows you
to run virtual machines

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:14.630
atop of Google's massive
infrastructure, so tons

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:20.360
and tons of computing cores,
of memory, of disk storage,

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:23.330
and of network,
globally distributed,

00:30:23.330 --> 00:30:27.140
and a tooling and workflow
that allows you to easily spin

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:32.210
up one to hundreds to thousands
to tens of thousands of VMs

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:34.790
and coordinate their
activity, and also

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:36.950
intelligently route
traffic to the instances

00:30:36.950 --> 00:30:40.250
based on your users needs.

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:41.760
We talked about
virtual machines.

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:44.250
There's a range
of machine types,

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:47.330
so you can go from
one CPU to 64 CPUs.

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:49.280
You can go from roughly
half a gig of RAM

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:52.220
to 416 gigabytes
of RAM, and they've

00:30:52.220 --> 00:30:55.290
got a set of different
combinations of resources.

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:57.890
So they've got four
preconfigured sets

00:30:57.890 --> 00:30:59.810
of recipes that
are standard, which

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:04.160
is a nice blend or balance
between CPU and memory.

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:07.880
They've got high CPU recipes
, where that's double the CPU

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:09.350
for a given amount of memory.

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:11.750
They have the inverse, which
is high memory instances,

00:31:11.750 --> 00:31:13.541
and that's like double
the amount of memory

00:31:13.541 --> 00:31:15.200
for a given amount of CPU.

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:16.880
They've got shared
CPUs, which are

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:20.300
the least expensive and
lowest performing VMs,

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:22.640
And then if none of these
preconfigured recipes

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:26.390
work for you, you can make
your own custom recipes

00:31:26.390 --> 00:31:27.920
and, basically,
pick how much CPU

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:30.290
and how much memory you want.

00:31:30.290 --> 00:31:32.570
For every VM that
you start up, you're

00:31:32.570 --> 00:31:36.680
going to need attached
storage, and so GCE offers

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:38.990
standard and SSD
persistent disks.

00:31:38.990 --> 00:31:42.020
Standard disks are
rotating magnetic media,

00:31:42.020 --> 00:31:46.120
and SSDs are obviously
really good for random I/O

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:48.230
kind of operations.

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:50.720
Disk options, whether
they're SSD or persistent,

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:53.330
offer you very high
availability and durability.

00:31:53.330 --> 00:31:56.520
Your data's is actually
spread across multiple disks,

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:59.690
so if a particular
device goes down,

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:03.200
your data does not get lost,
and you continue to have access.

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.840
Your data is encrypted
at REST, so Google

00:32:05.840 --> 00:32:08.810
automatically encrypts the
data and manages the keys.

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:10.850
If you work in an
industry where you're

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:12.770
required to manage
your own keys,

00:32:12.770 --> 00:32:14.330
you can also provide
your own keys

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:17.400
and manage the
encryption that way.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:19.110
They optimize your
disk performance,

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:21.390
so if you get a 10
terabyte volume.

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:24.240
That's actually probably a bunch
of hundred gigabyte volumes,

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:25.860
and your data is
striped across that

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:28.920
so you don't have to do any
software RAID configurations.

00:32:28.920 --> 00:32:31.680
And they have snapshots, which
allow you to easily back up

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:34.350
disks and make copies and then
transport that backup maybe

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:38.490
to other zones or other
regions to spin up instances.

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:42.270
Virtual machines range
from 8/10 of a cent

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:44.370
to $4 per instance hour.

00:32:44.370 --> 00:32:48.420
You only get charged while
the machine is running,

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:50.970
and there are a number of
really customer friendly pricing

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:53.040
innovations.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:56.140
Instances are billed
on a per minute basis,

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:59.220
so for instance, if you use
an instance for 11 minutes,

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:01.500
even if it's a $4
an hour instance,

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:04.902
you're only going to
pay roughly 1/6 of that.

00:33:04.902 --> 00:33:07.110
You're only going to pay
for the 11 minutes you used.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:09.060
There is a 10 minute
minimum, but you

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:11.070
don't pay for the whole
hour even if you only

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:12.840
use a fraction of an hour.

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:15.390
There are sustained
use discounts.

00:33:15.390 --> 00:33:19.180
What this means is, if you use
an instance 24 hours a day,

00:33:19.180 --> 00:33:21.150
seven days a week,
for the entire month,

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:23.700
you'll actually
get a 30% discount.

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:28.110
The more you use it, the bigger
the marginal discount becomes.

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:30.150
They even go so
far as to give you

00:33:30.150 --> 00:33:31.870
what are called
inferred instances,

00:33:31.870 --> 00:33:34.530
so this is, if you run one
instance for two weeks,

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:36.980
and you run another instance
for another two weeks,

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:38.730
they'll actually stitch
those two together

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:40.730
and say, oh, you ran an
instance for four weeks,

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:42.800
and they'll give
you the discount.

00:33:42.800 --> 00:33:46.490
And they also have this thing
called preemptable instances,

00:33:46.490 --> 00:33:47.720
which is--

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:50.340
these are instances that you
can get at a huge discount.

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:54.020
They're 80% less expensive, so
you only pay 20% of the price,

00:33:54.020 --> 00:33:57.510
but Google reserves the right
to claw them back at any time.

00:33:57.510 --> 00:33:59.870
So what that means is, if
you have a compute load,

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:03.440
for instance, where you
need 10 VMs running,

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:08.150
but you'd like 100, you can pay
for 10 regular instances and 90

00:34:08.150 --> 00:34:09.980
preemptable instances.

00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:12.823
And if, for some reason,
they take back 20% or 30%

00:34:12.823 --> 00:34:14.239
of those instances,
you still have

00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:16.040
a bunch of instances
that were running

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:17.570
at a vastly reduced cost.

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:23.000
We showed you, during the
demo, boot disk images,

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:24.449
and there are two
types of images.

00:34:24.449 --> 00:34:26.600
There are public images,
and these are maintained

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:29.030
by Google or third parties.

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:31.429
And these are clean operating
system boot images--

00:34:31.429 --> 00:34:33.909
so Debian, Core OS, Ubuntu.

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:35.989
There are also
premium public images,

00:34:35.989 --> 00:34:39.179
so like Red Hat Enterprise
Linux or SQL Server

00:34:39.179 --> 00:34:40.580
on Windows Server.

00:34:40.580 --> 00:34:43.730
These, you have to pay
an additional licensing

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:47.719
fee on top of the
VM instance fees

00:34:47.719 --> 00:34:51.659
because you're using third
party licensable software.

00:34:51.659 --> 00:34:53.040
Generally, what
happens is you'll

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:55.020
start with a public
image, and then you'll

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.000
install your own software.

00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:59.760
You'll change the
configuration, and then

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:01.347
you'll save that
as a custom image,

00:35:01.347 --> 00:35:02.930
and that's what we
did in the example.

00:35:02.930 --> 00:35:06.900
So we use our custom front end
and our custom back end images

00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:10.260
in order to create
instant templates

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:12.930
and then autoscaling
instance groups.

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:15.570
So that is customizing
a public image.

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:19.230
You can also use the new
Google VM migration service.

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:21.870
This was just launched in
the last couple of weeks

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:24.300
and it allows you
to actually migrate

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:27.030
live running VMs from
other environments

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:30.070
to GCP with no downtime.

00:35:30.070 --> 00:35:34.380
So check that out, or you
can also manually take, say,

00:35:34.380 --> 00:35:38.730
AWS virtual machines, export
them as raw disk images,

00:35:38.730 --> 00:35:42.270
and then tweak them and
import them into GCP

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:48.000
and then run those VMs now
in Google Compute Engine.

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:50.430
We had instance groups, and
again, an instance group

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:52.560
is simply a
collection of machines

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:55.770
that you can manage together
as opposed to individually.

00:35:55.770 --> 00:36:00.120
So these allow you to easily
add or remove instances.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:02.440
You can simply change the
size of the instance group,

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:05.380
and it can spin up
additional instances.

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:07.340
Gives you self-healing
on your instances,

00:36:07.340 --> 00:36:10.900
so if for some reason
an instance goes down

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:12.610
and you have a
health check running,

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:15.640
the instance group can
determine that that instance is

00:36:15.640 --> 00:36:16.750
no longer healthy.

00:36:16.750 --> 00:36:19.690
It can shut it down, and then
bring up a replacement instance

00:36:19.690 --> 00:36:21.850
with the same configuration.

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:25.630
We saw autoscaling, and this
can be based on CPU utilization.

00:36:25.630 --> 00:36:28.010
It can be based on
requests per second

00:36:28.010 --> 00:36:30.280
if it's HTTP load
balancing, or it

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.620
can be based on custom
Stackdriver metrics.

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:35.500
So you can basically see how
the machines are being used,

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:38.050
and once they exceed
an average utilization,

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:39.920
you can add
additional instances.

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:43.810
This also scales down, so if you
have five of those transcoding

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:46.600
servers, and then there's
no more transcoding to do,

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:49.810
after 10 minutes, it will start
backing off and shutting down

00:36:49.810 --> 00:36:51.250
instances.

00:36:51.250 --> 00:36:52.360
We saw load balancing.

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:55.150
And we built an
HTTP load balancer,

00:36:55.150 --> 00:36:59.110
which not only distributes the
load across multiple back end

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:02.200
instance groups, but it
intelligently routes traffic

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:05.350
to the nearest or
fastest instance group

00:37:05.350 --> 00:37:07.090
as long as it has capacity.

00:37:07.090 --> 00:37:09.520
There's also TCP and
UDP load balancing

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:12.220
options available in GCE.

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:14.650
And then these instance groups
can be zonal or regional.

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:17.890
So a zonal instance
group is a set

00:37:17.890 --> 00:37:21.340
of instances that are all in
the same sort of fault space.

00:37:21.340 --> 00:37:24.640
That means they're all in the
same building in a data center.

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:27.490
It's possible that that
zone could go down,

00:37:27.490 --> 00:37:31.630
so power could go out or a
network cable could go down.

00:37:31.630 --> 00:37:34.180
You can choose to make those
instance groups regional

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:36.460
instead, and if you do
that, your instances

00:37:36.460 --> 00:37:39.250
are spread across
multiple zones, which

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:41.991
makes it less likely that, if
there's an outage in one zone,

00:37:41.991 --> 00:37:44.365
that that's going to interrupt
service to your end users.

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:50.900
You have a sophisticated
range of networking options

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:53.930
available to you in
Google Compute Engine.

00:37:53.930 --> 00:37:56.300
It automatically creates
a network for you

00:37:56.300 --> 00:37:57.800
when you create a project.

00:37:57.800 --> 00:38:00.470
That network has a
variety of subnets,

00:38:00.470 --> 00:38:03.690
and those subnets are all
routed for you automatically.

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:05.870
So it automatically
generates these routes

00:38:05.870 --> 00:38:09.960
that allow all of these discrete
subnets to talk to one another.

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:13.640
You can also create custom
routes as necessary.

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:15.920
The firewall rules
are super flexible,

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:21.350
so basically, you can say that
instances with particular tabs

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:25.010
allow incoming traffic, whereas
instances without those tags

00:38:25.010 --> 00:38:26.960
don't allow incoming traffic.

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:30.260
By default, there are a
half dozen firewall rules

00:38:30.260 --> 00:38:32.780
created for you, but
you can tweak those

00:38:32.780 --> 00:38:35.040
and add as many as you want.

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:41.090
You have the ability to create
VPNs so that your private IP

00:38:41.090 --> 00:38:43.520
ranges within your
company are considered

00:38:43.520 --> 00:38:48.020
to be part of the GCP
private address namespace,

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:50.280
or the routing happens
between the two of them,

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:52.760
so you can easily work
in an environment that

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:57.050
bridges your GCP networks
and your internal networks.

00:38:57.050 --> 00:38:58.670
Again, we talked
about load balancing,

00:38:58.670 --> 00:39:00.620
and you also have
cloud DNS, which

00:39:00.620 --> 00:39:04.640
is GCP hosted public
facing DNS, if you

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:08.990
wanted to do DNS on the cloud.

00:39:08.990 --> 00:39:10.700
In terms of managing
all this, you

00:39:10.700 --> 00:39:13.520
saw me work primarily
with the cloud console,

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:15.950
so I did everything
sort of manually.

00:39:15.950 --> 00:39:17.570
As it turns out,
all work that you

00:39:17.570 --> 00:39:20.630
do in administering Google
Compute Engine is done

00:39:20.630 --> 00:39:24.530
through a standard set of APIs,
and when I'm using the console,

00:39:24.530 --> 00:39:26.690
the console is
talking to those APIs.

00:39:26.690 --> 00:39:31.040
I could also use a command line
utility, like gcloud or gsutil.

00:39:31.040 --> 00:39:34.130
Gcloud is for working with
Compute Engine resources.

00:39:34.130 --> 00:39:38.550
Gsutil is for working with
Google Cloud Storage resources.

00:39:38.550 --> 00:39:42.050
All of these basically
talk to the back end APIs.

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:43.910
There are third party
utilities, or you

00:39:43.910 --> 00:39:46.640
can write your own custom
scripts or applications

00:39:46.640 --> 00:39:50.410
to manage your
resources as well.

00:39:50.410 --> 00:39:53.910
So the question is, given all
of these capabilities, given

00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:57.480
the range of instance types,
given the instance groups

00:39:57.480 --> 00:40:02.160
and load balancing and so forth,
what use cases can you tackle,

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:05.820
and what could you do
with Compute Engine?

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:08.970
Here's one example
for inspiration.

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:13.530
Frame Store is a
company that does

00:40:13.530 --> 00:40:16.560
3D renderings and
video renderings,

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:20.280
and basically, they use
Compute Engine for excess burst

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:21.330
rendering capacity.

00:40:21.330 --> 00:40:24.150
So they have a big
render farm on site,

00:40:24.150 --> 00:40:27.150
but occasionally,
they need to have

00:40:27.150 --> 00:40:30.330
a whole bunch more cores thrown
at a distributed rendering

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:31.260
process.

00:40:31.260 --> 00:40:33.540
And so they work
with Compute Engine,

00:40:33.540 --> 00:40:38.970
and they can spin up to 15,000
cores at the drop of a hat.

00:40:38.970 --> 00:40:41.910
This actually allows them
to dramatically reduce

00:40:41.910 --> 00:40:44.070
the rendering time,
and that allows

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:47.250
them to do a ton of
fast turnarounds.

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:49.290
So they can actually
be more creative

00:40:49.290 --> 00:40:51.690
as well as hit
deadlines more easily,

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:55.050
and they save money doing it,
considerable amounts of money

00:40:55.050 --> 00:40:58.920
relative to having the same
sort of capacity on site.

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:01.680
This is a big example
of the little example

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:07.270
that we did with the transcoding
on the back end of our LMS.

00:41:07.270 --> 00:41:10.900
Now, after today, when you start
working with Compute Engine,

00:41:10.900 --> 00:41:14.380
you may need to go find answers
to specific questions or guides

00:41:14.380 --> 00:41:16.450
on how to perform
certain activities,

00:41:16.450 --> 00:41:19.150
so let me give you a few
hints on where to look

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:20.890
for additional information.

00:41:20.890 --> 00:41:22.930
Start with search.

00:41:22.930 --> 00:41:24.830
Search is your best friend.

00:41:24.830 --> 00:41:29.070
So if you start with GCP space
and your topic of instance--

00:41:29.070 --> 00:41:32.260
or interest, so for instance,
GCP instance billing

00:41:32.260 --> 00:41:33.880
will give you a
bunch of articles.

00:41:33.880 --> 00:41:36.250
The top one or two
links will almost always

00:41:36.250 --> 00:41:39.670
be directly relevant and
exactly what you want.

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:42.250
If you're trying to figure out
how to use the command line,

00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:45.400
always start with
gcloud compute resource,

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:51.830
so gcloud compute instances,
gcloud compute instance groups,

00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:53.050
autoscalers.

00:41:53.050 --> 00:41:55.270
Whenever you want to manage
one of those pieces using

00:41:55.270 --> 00:41:58.300
the command line, as
I was using the UI,

00:41:58.300 --> 00:42:03.190
you would look for the
gcloud compute command,

00:42:03.190 --> 00:42:05.950
and again, it will
give you what you need.

00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:09.574
The documentation is
actually pretty good.

00:42:09.574 --> 00:42:10.990
Typically, when
you search, you're

00:42:10.990 --> 00:42:13.770
going to end up in some sort
of a how-to guide or a Quick

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:16.501
Start, but if you look
at the breadcrumbs

00:42:16.501 --> 00:42:19.000
at the top of the article, you
can actually go up to the top

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:23.320
where you'll see a top
level table of contents.

00:42:23.320 --> 00:42:26.940
And this is relatively standard
across all the products,

00:42:26.940 --> 00:42:30.020
so once you master this
for GCE for instance,

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:32.380
you can use it for App Engine
or Google Cloud Storage

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:33.580
or whatever.

00:42:33.580 --> 00:42:35.620
So there will be
Quick Starts, and this

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:38.500
is a very short exercise that
gets your hands dirty and gets

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:40.600
you up and running
on the technology.

00:42:40.600 --> 00:42:42.860
There are how-to guides,
so this for instance,

00:42:42.860 --> 00:42:46.000
would have guides on how
do you build an autoscaler,

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:48.700
how do you create an image,
how do you create an instance

00:42:48.700 --> 00:42:51.160
group, how do you
create a load balancer,

00:42:51.160 --> 00:42:55.900
and then there are deep
dives into APIs and concepts

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:58.680
and so forth.

00:42:58.680 --> 00:43:02.820
A few notes on how
to best evaluate

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:04.680
how well Compute
Engine will serve

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:09.310
your needs for particular use
cases in your environment.

00:43:09.310 --> 00:43:10.800
So the first thing
to consider is,

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:13.920
when you're trying to make
a decision about applying

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:17.850
a new technology to
business opportunities,

00:43:17.850 --> 00:43:19.510
you want to take it seriously.

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:23.340
It's not hard, but have a
process and follow the process.

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:25.680
Typically, what we
recommend to people

00:43:25.680 --> 00:43:28.710
is that you start by defining
some decision criteria.

00:43:28.710 --> 00:43:31.800
What is it that you
need to know in order

00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:34.140
to make a decision that,
yes, this technology will

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:35.530
work for you?

00:43:35.530 --> 00:43:40.410
So for instance, does it scale
to meet our company's usage

00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:41.340
needs?

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:43.020
Is it easy to configure?

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:45.630
Can our existing systems
operational professionals

00:43:45.630 --> 00:43:48.640
get up to speed within
a couple of days?

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:51.450
Once you've decided what
it is you need to know,

00:43:51.450 --> 00:43:53.400
then you figure out how to test.

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:55.500
So you're going to take
an existing use case,

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:59.760
and generally, you're going to
create a minimum viable product

00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:02.370
that emulates either
something you're already doing

00:44:02.370 --> 00:44:04.500
or something that
you hope to do.

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:06.296
And once you have
figured out how to test--

00:44:06.296 --> 00:44:07.920
so what is that you're
going to build--

00:44:07.920 --> 00:44:09.570
then you do some
research to figure out

00:44:09.570 --> 00:44:12.784
exactly how to architect
that on the platform.

00:44:12.784 --> 00:44:14.950
Then you're going to actually
go ahead and build it.

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:16.690
You also need to
decide who's going

00:44:16.690 --> 00:44:18.869
to be on the team and
their key players.

00:44:18.869 --> 00:44:20.410
You need to have
somebody who's going

00:44:20.410 --> 00:44:22.030
to be responsible
for the architecture

00:44:22.030 --> 00:44:22.821
of the application.

00:44:22.821 --> 00:44:25.060
So for instance, are
you using Cloud SQL

00:44:25.060 --> 00:44:27.576
as part of the solution?

00:44:27.576 --> 00:44:29.200
How are you going to
do load balancing?

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:31.017
Where are you going
to locate servers?

00:44:31.017 --> 00:44:32.350
You need some operations people.

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:34.266
These are the people who
are going to actually

00:44:34.266 --> 00:44:37.152
be standing up the instances
and managing the configuration.

00:44:37.152 --> 00:44:39.610
You need somebody who's going
to be responsible for program

00:44:39.610 --> 00:44:41.669
managing and then
reporting out the results,

00:44:41.669 --> 00:44:43.210
and then obviously,
you need somebody

00:44:43.210 --> 00:44:45.910
in the business side of things
to help with the decision

00:44:45.910 --> 00:44:47.380
making.

00:44:47.380 --> 00:44:51.280
Typically, we find like
one month per use case

00:44:51.280 --> 00:44:53.030
is a pretty good time period.

00:44:53.030 --> 00:44:57.070
So in the first week, you do
your defining of the criteria,

00:44:57.070 --> 00:44:58.780
you recruit your
team, and you agree

00:44:58.780 --> 00:45:00.190
on what you're going to test.

00:45:00.190 --> 00:45:03.010
In weeks two and three, you
define your architecture,

00:45:03.010 --> 00:45:06.647
and you actually
perform some pre-testing

00:45:06.647 --> 00:45:08.980
to make sure that you understand
how all the pieces work

00:45:08.980 --> 00:45:10.270
and you're doing things right.

00:45:10.270 --> 00:45:12.644
And then in the last week,
you actually perform the test,

00:45:12.644 --> 00:45:15.190
consolidate the results,
and you make a decision.

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:16.630
Now, obviously,
this timeline will

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:22.000
change depending on the
complexity of your use cases

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:23.601
as well as the
investment that you've

00:45:23.601 --> 00:45:25.600
put in terms of how many
people are on the team.

00:45:25.600 --> 00:45:30.400
This could be longer or shorter,
but two to three month trial

00:45:30.400 --> 00:45:32.350
period will give
you the opportunity

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:34.630
to test a couple of
different use cases

00:45:34.630 --> 00:45:38.170
and make a really intelligent
decision about how to apply GCE

00:45:38.170 --> 00:45:40.460
to your particular use cases.

00:45:40.460 --> 00:45:42.820
So hopefully, that
gives you a good sense

00:45:42.820 --> 00:45:46.910
of how to go about
evaluating Google Compute

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:51.480
Engine for specific use
cases in your environment.

00:45:51.480 --> 00:45:53.300
And that's all for this session.

